KW  - Urban flooding prediction
KW  - Machine learning surrogate modeling
KW  - Automated machine learning
KW  - Explainable AI
AB  - Intensified urban pluvial flood risks highlight the urgent need for accurate, efficient, and transparent forecasting tools. However, the black-box nature of current machine learning surrogates limits their interpretability regarding key drivers. In this context, this study proposes a novel framework that integrates Automated Machine Learning (AutoML), SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP), and Generalized Additive Models (GAM). Using Beijing's Shijingshan District as a case study, the AutoML-optimized XGBoost generalizes well to unseen rainstorms, achieving a 2000-fold speedup over MIKE FLOOD. The integrated SHAP–GAM analysis reveals nonlinear responses that align with hydrological expectations, reinforcing the credibility of the learned decision behavior. Depth-stratified analysis further shows stage-specific dynamics: elevation, topographic wetness index, and manhole density form a core driver set (contributing over 43% across depth bins), with their hierarchy reorganizing as severity increases. Overall, by integrating predictive power with interpretability, the proposed framework enables rapid and transparent urban flood forecasting and management.
