AB  - The GRACE (-FO) missions captured crucial gravity-based signals of Earth's terrestrial mass shifts, but an 11-month gap and isolated data lapses disrupted its continuity, complicating its robust reconstruction. To address this, we propose a knowledge-guided hybrid framework integrating domain-informed feature engineering with multiscale nonlinear learning. We developed two hybrid models, Coupled Empirical Orthogonal Function–Random Forest (CEOF-RF) and Temporally Segmented- Spline Extreme Gradient Boosting (TS-SplineX), and evaluated them alongside standalone models via a Monte Carlo-based remove-and-restore framework. The CEOF-RF combines orthogonal function-based spatial mode extraction with nonlinear learning, allowing physically coherent and multivariate-aware reconstructions. In contrast, TS-SplineX employs wavelet-spline interpolation followed by XGBoost, capturing local anomalies, seasonal discontinuities, and nonlinear dependencies. TS-SplineX with ReliefF-derived features showed consistent performance (RMSE: 0.225 cm; r > 0.97; NSE >0.94), without adding artefactual trends. This study offers an adaptable and interpretable reconstruction framework for satellite data assimilation and multi-region hydrological analysis in climate-vulnerable regions.
ER  - 

