KW  - Flood forecasting
KW  - Ensemble Kalman filter
KW  - Data assimilation
KW  - Pearl river network
KW  - Hydrodynamic model
AB  - Accurate prediction of water levels is imperative for navigation safety, flood mitigation, and infrastructure management; however, predictive capability is often compromised by rapidly evolving hydrological conditions and boundary uncertainties. To address these challenges, this study develops a data assimilation framework coupling a one-dimensional hydrodynamic river network model with the Ensemble Kalman Filter (EnKF). The proposed framework dynamically identifies Manning's coefficients under uncertain boundary inputs while ensuring numerical stability and hydraulic consistency. Validated in the Pearl River Basin, the system assimilates in-situ water-level observations to generate forecasts across multiple lead times (1–24 h) over a continuous 30-day (720 h) simulation. Results demonstrate that the EnKF-based assimilation effectively mitigates systematic biases and sustains stable forecasting performance, achieving an average Nash–Sutcliffe Efficiency (NSE) of 0.85 across 33 stations. These findings demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed framework for water-level forecasting under complex boundary conditions within the examined case study.
ER  - 

